
root = true

[*]
charset = utf-8
end_of_line = crlf
insert_final_newline = true
trim_trailing_whitespace = true

[*.cs]
indent_style = space
indent_size = 4
tab_width = 4

# Braces and namespaces
csharp_prefer_braces = true:warning
csharp_style_namespace_declarations = file_scoped:suggestion

# C# conventions
csharp_using_directive_placement = outside_namespace
dotnet_sort_system_directives_first = true
dotnet_style_require_accessibility_modifiers = for_non_interface_members:warning

# Prefer modern C# syntax
csharp_style_var_for_built_in_types = true:suggestion
csharp_style_var_when_type_is_apparent = true:suggestion
csharp_style_var_elsewhere = false:suggestion

# Simplify code
dotnet_style_object_initializer = true:suggestion
dotnet_style_collection_initializer = true:suggestion
dotnet_style_readonly_field = true:suggestion
dotnet_style_prefer_auto_properties = true:suggestion

[*.xaml]
indent_style = space
indent_size = 4
```

These are sensible defaults for a modern C# project. EditorConfig controls code conventions, while the VS Code settings control editor behavior. <Cite refs={["turn292650search0","turn292650search2"]}/>

## 4. Format the entire project

Formatting on save only handles files you save. To format all supported files in the project, run this from the terminal:

```powershell
dotnet format
```

To check formatting without modifying files:

```powershell
dotnet format --verify-no-changes
```

You can also enable code-style diagnostics during builds by adding this property to your `.csproj`:

```xml
<PropertyGroup>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
</PropertyGroup>
```

This enables applicable .NET code-style rules during builds, based on their configured severities. <Cite refs={["turn292650search1","turn292650search7"]}/>

**My recommendation:** Commit `.editorconfig` and `.vscode/settings.json` to Git. That way, your formatting and editor defaults travel with the project, while your personal VS Code settings remain separate.
